---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";
import { noScore } from "../../../lib/cases";

const card = archiveAi["case-kept"];

const record = `<p class="maker">Émilie du Pré</p>
<p class="object-count"></p>`;

const layers = [
  { name: "Raw response", holds: "The markup, accents and the blank count, as received", verdict: ["Full replay", "is-ok"] },
  { name: "Extracted fields", holds: "The fields pulled from the markup, with the count still blank", verdict: ["Last before the bug", "is-ok"], last: true },
  { name: "Parsed counts", holds: "A 0 where the count was blank", verdict: ["Carries it", "is-bad"], bug: true },
  { name: "Published catalog", holds: "Zero objects, where the partner supplied no count", verdict: ["Carries it", "is-bad"] },
];

const merges = [
  { from: ["blank count", "literal 0"], to: "0" },
  { from: ["Émilie du Pré", "another original spelling"], to: "Emilie du Pre" },
];

const receipt = [
  ["Requested URL and final URL if redirected", "Shows what the museum asked for and where the response came from."],
  ["Fetch timestamp in UTC", "Places the response in the nightly history and helps match it to logs."],
  ["Import run ID", "Connects the file to other pages from the same run and to the catalog built from them."],
  ["HTTP status", "Helps distinguish a successful download from a saved error response."],
  ["Reported content type and encoding, when provided", "Helps a future importer interpret the saved bytes, including characters such as É."],
  ["File size and SHA-256 hash", "Help check whether the archived bytes still match what was originally saved."],
];

const checks = [
  ["A crash", "The importer crashes halfway through tonight’s download processing. What can the museum recover if it saved the raw response before cleaning started?"],
  ["Where to replay from", "If the count parser has the blank-to-zero bug, which saved copy is the quickest starting point for verifying the fix, and why can’t the published catalog serve as that input?"],
  ["A new question", "A curator wants to know whether the partner marked a maker’s name as uncertain last year. The published catalog contains only standardized names. Where would you look, and what would you look for?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("case-kept")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's raw-zone study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 4 and place one more bug on the grid: draws scored two points, not one, with bug 3 still in archive (a)."
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">What a museum's archive must keep</h2>
  <p>Every night, a museum downloads collection records from a partner’s website. One night, the partner sends a record that looks like this:</p>
  <div class="pr-code"><p class="pr-label">The partner's record</p><pre>{record}</pre></div>
  <p>The museum’s import strips the HTML, standardizes the maker’s name, and parses the object count. A bug turns the blank count into <code>0</code>, so the catalog reports that the museum has zero objects where the partner supplied no count.</p>

  <h2>1. Save the raw response first</h2>
  <p>The <strong>raw zone</strong>, also called the <strong>bronze layer</strong> in this archive, holds the response body as the museum received it, along with a small record of the fetch. It keeps the markup, accents, blank fields, and any malformed records.</p>
  <p>The museum writes this copy before cleaning starts. If the importer crashes or quietly changes a value, the original input is already saved. Downloading the same URL tomorrow may give the museum a different version of the record.</p>

  <h2>2. Replay from the last layer before the damage</h2>
  <p>Suppose the museum saves a copy after each step:</p>
  <ol class="pa-ladder">
    {layers.map((l) => (
      <li class:list={[{ "is-last": l.last, "is-bug": l.bug, "is-carry": l.verdict[1] === "is-bad" }]}>
        {l.bug && <p class="pa-ladder-step">The count parser's bug runs here</p>}
        <p class="pa-ladder-head"><b>{l.name}</b><span class:list={["pr-chip", l.verdict[1]]}>{l.verdict[0]}</span></p>
        <p class="pa-ladder-holds">{l.holds}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p>The <strong>extracted fields</strong> are the last saved copy before the bug. They still contain the blank, so the museum can feed them into the fixed count parser and check that the result is missing, then run the downstream steps again. A test record with an explicit <code>0</code> should still produce zero.</p>
  <p>The <strong>raw response</strong> can also verify the fix by replaying the whole import. The <strong>old parsed counts</strong> and <strong>published catalog</strong> already carry the incorrect zero. They help show what went wrong and compare old and corrected results. On their own, they cannot tell the fixed parser which zeros came from blanks.</p>

  <h2>3. Keep information you might need later</h2>
  <p class="pr-pull">When different inputs produce the same output, the output alone cannot tell you which input you started with.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-merges">
    {merges.map((m) => (
      <div class="pa-merge">
        <div class="pa-merge-in">{m.from.map((f) => <p><code>{f}</code></p>)}</div>
        <p class="pr-arrow"><span>import</span></p>
        <div class="pa-merge-out"><p><code>{m.to}</code><span>which one was it?</span></p></div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </figure>
  <p>An empty field and a literal <code>0</code> both became <code>0</code>. Two original spellings might become <code>Emilie du Pre</code>. Different HTML tags and attributes can produce the same plain text.</p>
  <p>The raw copy also preserves evidence for questions the museum hasn’t thought to ask. Months later, a curator might ask when the partner began marking some maker names with a <code>data-attribution="uncertain"</code> attribute. The museum can inspect its archived responses to see when that attribute first appeared in a nightly download, even if the importer never extracted it.</p>

  <h2>4. Save a small fetch record beside each file</h2>
  <div class="pr-scroll">
    <table class="pr-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Why keep it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{receipt.map(([f, w]) => <tr><th scope="row">{f}</th><td>{w}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>The museum's blank count is {noScore.home} vs. {noScore.away} on {noScore.day}: a bare “Final” with no score, which the parsed-rows archive would record as a scoreless draw.</p>
    <p>One difference decides Case 2, whose three archives are ones Kick could have written. In the museum, the bug is in a step after extraction, so the extracted fields are the last layer before the damage. In Case 2 the tidy rule runs inside the parser, so the parsed rows would already carry it. The tidied HTML looks raw, but its tidy step runs before saving, so it would be a cleaned copy too. Ask of any bug which step did the damage, then look for the last copy saved before that step.</p>
    <p>The reply's fetch record is what Case 2 calls the sidecar.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>5. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-ladder { display: grid; gap: 0.45rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .pa-ladder li { padding: 0.55rem 0.85rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-left: 4px solid var(--tl-raw-edge); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-card); }
  .pa-ladder li.is-last { border-left-color: var(--pr-green); box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--pr-green); }
  .pa-ladder li.is-carry { border-left-color: var(--pr-red); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ladder p { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ladder p:last-child { margin: 0; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ladder-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.3rem 0.6rem; }
  .pa-ladder-head b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ladder-holds { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ladder-step { color: var(--pr-red); font-size: 0.76rem !important; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  :global(:root:not([data-theme="light"])) :global(.pr) .pa-ladder-step { color: #ef7b68; }
  .pa-merges { display: grid; gap: 0.8rem; }
  .pa-merge { display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-merge { grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr; } }
  .pa-merge-in, .pa-merge-out { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-merge-in p, :global(.pr) .pa-merge-out p { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0; padding: 0.4rem 0.7rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); font-size: 0.88rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-merge-out p { border: 1.5px dashed var(--pr-red); }
  .pa-merge-out span { color: var(--tl-muted); }
</style>
